00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.789 align:start position:0%
bioinformatics is the intersection of  informatics or the science of processing  data for storage and retrieval  and biology the study of living  organisms  this field developed tremendously in the  last several decades transforming life  sciences  enabling biotechnology and empowering  biomedical research  as a result came many new discoveries  that include new treatments  diagnostic assays and design strategies  for experimentation  importantly today there is a growing  appreciation among biologists and  clinicians for data  this includes new types of data  generation computational methods for  data  organization and analysis as well as  visualization approaches that show  statistical patterns in the context of  biological phenomena  in this video i will introduce you to  these important terms and topics which  we will discuss throughout the full  course  as a result of completing this course  you will see the broad applications of  next generation sequencing  by statistical methods of analysis as  well as computational tools that are  used to process  and mine big data moreover you will have  a clear understanding of the logic  behind bioinformatics analysis  in the context of cutting-edge research  projects and see how data science  methods can be used to study biological  phenomena  in conclusion you will also be able to  plan  how to take what you have learned and  apply it to an independent research  project  using tools and methods that we cover so  what is bioinformatics it is a  convergence of biology and data  where both are equally important as we  will see in the next few slides  who uses bioinformatics and who is it  for the answer  might have previously been statisticians  computer scientists or engineers  but the truth is that today more and  more biologists and clinicians are  expected to understand  as well as handle their own data to  publish and succeed  in both basic and translational research  projects  in fact biology itself is rapidly  acquiring the character of a data  science  this science is based on high throughput  technologies  that generate billions of data points  from genes proteins and other molecules  which characterize biological systems  and biological phenomena these data are  compiled  into large repositories that can be  systematically studied  to understand interrogate and establish  trends for many different applications  is such data available yes one such  repository  is maintained by the national center for  biotechnology information  which has a collection of peer-reviewed  articles biological data sets  and projects on various topics as you  can see  in the past several decades this  resource has been growing exponentially  today there are over 80 000 publicly  available data sets  in just one part of ncbi called geo  or gene expression omnibus  such data repositories contain data sets  on  thousands of genes in each sample with  tens  or even hundreds of samples per data set  so what can be done with this data  anyone  including you can find a relevant data  set learn how to analyze it  and discover something new about the  world that we live in  that's why this course in bioinformatics  is a good place to start  it will provide you with a full roadmap  to go from a to z  or from basics to advanced and  cutting-edge bioinformatics methods  in the context of projects that you can  learn from  we call this roadmap omixlogic it  consists of online coursework  interactive tutorials as well as  projects and datasets from peer-reviewed  research articles to get started  you will need a solid overview of key  technologies that are used to generate  data  omix technologies these courses will  include  practical exercises to see how this data  can be analyzed  and guidance on developing a project of  your own  using various analytical tools and  publicly available biological data sets  in the next few lessons we will review  important terminology  provide perspectives from experts in  this exciting field  and review how quizzes allow you to see  what you have learned including  assignments for hands-on practice  in these assignments you will see how  the practical  analysis skills help find answers  to biological questions we'll start our  review  from an introduction to genomic data so  we will focus on next generation  sequencing to  find variation in genomics  then we will talk about transcriptomic  data  or rna-seq sequencing that allows us to  measure  different products of genes including  gene isoforms  and their levels of abundance and  alternative splicing  then we'll talk about epigenomic  regulation  and how we can study epigenetic  variation  or non-coding variants that lead to  different levels of expression patterns  focusing primarily on chromatin  immunoprecipitation  and whole genome by sulfate sequencing  data  we'll also talk about the microbiome and  the ability to study microorganisms  using metagenomic sequencing  including 16s ribosomal rna and  functional metagenomics  then we'll talk about biostatistics  coding  and machine learning in the context of  these important skills  you'll see how important it is to  understand what these methods do  how to visualize data and how to develop  good reproducible research techniques in  different coding libraries  and repositories the way these courses  are organized is that they provide you  with insights  into terminology and the biological  context  then provide you with a map of different  topics  an introduction to a data set or a  project  analysis techniques in the context of  interactive pipeline builders  and help monitor the progress with a  point system for every activity that you  do  the interactive analysis pipelines will  help establish  what's called a bioinformatics analysis  pipeline  logic so there you will see color-coded  maps  an explanation of methods using  informative pop-ups  a variety of input and output formats  for data analysis visualization and  interpretation  and help you develop a sense of a guided  process which will  lead to reproducible workflow analysis  that you can share  these types of pipelines will cover  major areas for sequence alignment  rna sequencing analysis evolutionary  studies  machine learning methods variant calling  and structure analysis  many of such methods are going to be  provided with the source code  where you will see how the methods can  be  implemented in different languages like  r and python  and how those could be changed by their  settings  and specific libraries for visualization  then we will see how experts use such  methodology  to answer biological research questions  so here we will introduce you to a  number of curated data sets  that you can use for practice and we  will use a review methodology where we  will take at both the  abstract the method section the results  and the discussion to think about what  the authors did  to produce the results that they have  found  as a result you will be able to look at  independent projects from  data sets that you have been studying  methods that you've learned  and combine them into novel projects  many of the students that have completed  these courses  have now gone on to present their own  research studies  using publicly available data sets and  the methods that we discuss in this  course  importantly you will see how  bioinformatics  allows you to leverage big data in a  variety of different contexts  to understand the relationship between  clinical and omix data  identify new strategies for therapeutic  intervention  and a novel diagnostic approach that  helps us understand disease  at the molecular level for the duration  of this course you will be using the t  bioinfo platform  for large data analysis and integration  of multi-omics data  and to learn all of the coding  techniques you will be introduced to the  omics logic code playground which allows  you to  practice and see the correct way to  write code in r  and python this course will  introduce you to bioinformatics where  you will see  processing bioinformatics data  visualization techniques  statistical analysis data mining and  biological interpretation  all with the goal to help you develop an  overview  of bioinformatics its applications and  how you personally can use these methods  for data analysis
 

